<span style="font-size:110%">'''Dispute overview'''</span>

There is currently a dispute as to how to describe the geography of Israel in the lead of the article. Originally, the article read that Israel shared borders with the West Bank and Gaza Strip (among other borders). Some editors have insisted on adding "the Palestinian territories (or State of Palestine) comprising the West Bank and Gaza Strip on the east and southwest respectively."

All reliable secondary sources put forward do not refer to the territories of the West Bank and Gaza as Palestine. In addition, encyclopedias and other sources that have country profiles for Israel do not refer to Israel as bordering "Palestine," nor do they have entries on any country called Palestine. (See, e.g., [http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/296740/Israel Encyclopedia Britannica];[http://education.yahoo.com/reference/encyclopedia/entry/Israel Encyclopedia Columbia]; [http://countrystudies.us/israel/34.htm Library of Congress Country Studies], [http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/world/countries/israel.html ''Washington Post'' Country Profiles]; [http://www.infoplease.com/country/israel.html#ixzz2sN1u9qQZ Infoplease]).

All sources indicate a Palestinian state is yet to be established. [http://bigstory.ap.org/article/palestinian-envoy-blasts-israel-claim-west-bank AP], [http://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/03/world/middleeast/palestinian-leader-seeks-nato-force-in-future-state.html?hpw&rref=world NY Times]. The sources likewise never use terms like "president of Palestine," etc. In addition, they identify incidents originating there as from the [http://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/world/middleeast/west-bank-funerals-become-displays-of-defiance.html West Bank] or Gaza Strip, never as Palestine.

Indeed, discussion of the prospects of Palestinian statehood is important, and it is included in the following paragraph, where it discusses the status of Israeli–Palestinian negotiations in the lead. Further explanation is included in the body. But the intro describing Israel's geography should be kept neutral and factual. Reliable secondary sources guide us and they are in agreement with their terminology.

