*'''A - Resignation from MIT and FSF'''
In September 2019, Stallman resigned from both MIT and the [[Free Software Foundation]] following his posts on an internal MIT [[CSAIL]] [[listserv]] mailing list about [[Jeffrey Epstein]]'s relationship with deceased MIT professor [[Marvin Minsky]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Richard M. Stallman resigns — Free Software Foundation — working together for free software|url=https://www.fsf.org/news/richard-m-stallman-resigns|access-date=2020-08-11|website=www.fsf.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Sweeney|first=Emily|last2=Ellement|first2=John R.|date=|title=Richard Stallman resigns from MIT after comments about Jeffrey Epstein|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2019/09/17/richard-stallman-resigns-from-mit-after-comments-about-jeffrey-epstein/MCj0sxJQCJiBoajIkijA3L/story.html|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=BostonGlobe.com|language=en-US|publication-date=September 17, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=2019: July - October Political Notes - Richard Stallman|url=https://stallman.org/archives/2019-jul-oct.html#16_September_2019_(Resignation)|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=stallman.org|quote=I am resigning effective immediately from my position in CSAIL at MIT.}}</ref> In response to a post citing an article about [[Virginia Giuffre]]'s deposition that Epstein had directed her, as a minor, to have sex with Minsky, Stallman criticized the choice of the word "assaulting", which implies the use of force, to describe Minsky's behavior.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Sweeney|first=Emily|last2=Ellement|first2=John R.|date=September 17, 2019|title=Richard Stallman resigns from MIT after comments about Jeffrey Epstein - The Boston Globe|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2019/09/17/richard-stallman-resigns-from-mit-after-comments-about-jeffrey-epstein/MCj0sxJQCJiBoajIkijA3L/story.html|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=|website=|language=en-US|quote=The injustice is in the word ‘assaulting.’ [...] The word ‘assaulting’ presumes that he applied force or violence, in some unspecified way, but the article itself says no such thing. Only that they had sex.}}</ref> In the emails, which were published to the public via [[Medium (website)|Medium]], Stallman also wrote: 

{{quote|Giuffre was being coerced into sex -- by Epstein. She was being harmed. But the details do affect whether, and to what extent, Minsky was responsible for that. […] We can imagine many scenarios, but the most plausible scenario is that she presented herself to him as entirely willing. Assuming she was being coerced by Epstein, he would have had every reason to tell her to conceal that from most of his associates.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sweeney|first=Emily|last2=Ellement|first2=John R.|date=|title=Richard Stallman resigns from MIT after comments about Jeffrey Epstein|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2019/09/17/richard-stallman-resigns-from-mit-after-comments-about-jeffrey-epstein/MCj0sxJQCJiBoajIkijA3L/story.html|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=BostonGlobe.com|language=en-US|publication-date=September 17, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Lee|first=Timothy B.|date=2019-09-17|title=Richard Stallman leaves MIT after controversial remarks on rape|url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/09/richard-stallman-leaves-mit-after-controversial-remarks-on-rape/|access-date=2020-08-11|website=Ars Technica|language=en-us}}</ref>|author=|title=|source=}}

Further down the thread, responding to an argument that the location and time assumed based on the article would make it [[statutory rape]], Stallman added: "It is morally absurd to define 'rape' in a way that depends on minor details such as [[Laws_regarding_rape#Laws_by_country|which country it was in]] or whether the victim was [[Ages of consent in the United States|18 years old or 17]]".<ref name=":0" /> Stallman's words were perceived by some as an attempt to downplay sexual exploitation and minimize Minsky's alleged  involvement.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Svrluga|first=Susan|date=September 17, 2019|title=Computer scientist Richard Stallman resigns from MIT after comments about Epstein scandal|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2019/09/17/computer-scientist-richard-stallman-resigns-mit-after-comments-about-epstein-scandal/|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=|website=Washington Post}}</ref> Reports also claimed that Stallman defended Epstein, which he denied: "Headlines say that I defended Epstein. [...] Nothing could be further from the truth. I've called him a 'serial rapist,' and said he deserved to be imprisoned".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Lee|first=Timothy B.|date=2019-09-17|title=Richard Stallman leaves MIT after controversial remarks on rape|url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/09/richard-stallman-leaves-mit-after-controversial-remarks-on-rape/|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-05|website=Ars Technica|language=en-us|quote=Stallman objected to headlines saying he defended Jeffrey Epstein."Headlines say that I defended Epstein," Stallman wrote. "Nothing could be further from the truth. I've called him a 'serial rapist,' and said he deserved to be imprisoned.}}</ref> 

Criticisms intensified when Stallman's earlier remarks about pedophilia resurfaced: "I am skeptical of the claim that voluntarily [sic] pedophilia harms children. The arguments that it causes harm seem to be based on cases which aren’t voluntary, which are then stretched by parents who are horrified by the idea that their little baby is maturing".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bort|first=Julie|date=|title=A programmer explains why he's willing to quit rather than work with industry legend Richard Stallman, who resigned from MIT after controversial remarks on Jeffrey Epstein|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/gnu-programmers-call-for-richard-stallman-to-quit-2019-10|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=Business Insider|quote=His remarks so disturbed MIT alum Selam Jie Gano [...] This included remarks by Stallman about pedophilia: "I am skeptical of the claim that voluntarily [sic] pedophilia harms children. The arguments that it causes harm seem to be based on cases which aren't voluntary, which are then stretched by parents who are horrified by the idea that their little baby is maturing."}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=2006: March - June Political Notes - Richard Stallman|url=https://stallman.org/archives/2006-mar-jun.html#05%20June%202006%20(Dutch%20paedophiles%20form%20political%20party)|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=stallman.org|quote=I am skeptical of the claim that voluntarily pedophilia harms children...}}</ref> In 2003, when commenting on judge [[William H. Pryor Jr.|William Pryor]]'s claim, in support of an anti-sodomy law, that a constitutional right to choose one's sexual partner must logically extend to "prostitution, adultery, necrophilia, bestiality, possession of child pornography, and even incest and pedophilia," Stallman wrote that those "should be legal as long as no one is coerced. They are illegal only because of prejudice and narrowmindedness".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Flaherty|first=Colleen|date=September 18, 2019|title=Computer scientist Richard Stallman leaves MIT amid controversial remarks about the Epstein case|url=https://www.insidehighered.com/news/2019/09/18/computer-scientist-richard-stallman-leaves-mit-amid-controversial-remarks-about|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=|website=Inside Higher Ed|language=en|quote=In another post from 2003, Stallman quoted then-federal appeals court nominee William Pryor's argument that if choice in sexual partners was guaranteed by the Constitution, then “adultery, necrophilia, bestiality, possession of child pornography, and even incest and pedophilia” also would be, as well. Stallman’s analysis was that Pryor was “probably mistaken, legally -- but that is unfortunate. All of these acts should be legal as long as no one is coerced. They are illegal only because of prejudice and narrow-mindedness.”}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Stallman |first1=Richard |title=Political notes from 2003: May - August |url=https://stallman.org/archives/2003-may-aug.html |website=stallman.org |publisher=stallman |accessdate=3 May 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Lawrence Hurley|date=19 May 2016|title=Trump's U.S. high court list: all conservative, some provocative|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/usa-election-trump-court-idUSL2N18G273|accessdate=7 June 2020|work=Reuters}}</ref> He has since disavowed this belief.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bort|first=Julie|date=|title=A programmer explains why he's willing to quit rather than work with industry legend Richard Stallman, who resigned from MIT after controversial remarks on Jeffrey Epstein|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/gnu-programmers-call-for-richard-stallman-to-quit-2019-10|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=Business Insider|quote=He is said to have later changed his mind about that position.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=2019: July - October Political Notes - Richard Stallman|url=https://stallman.org/archives/2019-jul-oct.html#14_September_2019_(Sex_between_an_adult_and_a_child_is_wrong)|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-11|website=stallman.org|quote=I've learned to understand how sex with a child can harm per psychologically. This changed my mind about the matter: I think adults should not do that.}}</ref>

A joint statement signed by 33 GNU project developers classified Stallman's behavior as being alienating and advocated his departure from the project.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Joint statement on the GNU Project|url=https://guix.gnu.org/blog/2019/joint-statement-on-the-gnu-project/|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-12|website=guix.gnu.org}}</ref> Nevertheless, Stallman remains the head of the [[GNU Project]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Richard Stallman To Continue As Head Of The GNU Project - Phoronix|url=https://www.phoronix.com/scan.php?page=news_item&px=Richard-Stallman-GNU-Head|access-date=2020-08-11|website=www.phoronix.com}}</ref><ref name="phoronix.com" />

| width="50%" align="{{{align|left}}}" valign="{{{valign|top}}}" style="border:1"|

*'''B - Resignation from MIT and FSF'''
In August and September 2019 it was learned that [[Jeffrey Epstein]] had made controversial donations to MIT, and in the wake of this, [[MIT Media Lab]] Director's [[Joi Ito]] resigned. Students and others users started an internal MIT [[CSAIL]] [[listserv]] mailing list thread to protest the coverup of MIT's connections to Epstein.<ref name="vice emailchain">{{Cite web|title=Famed Computer Scientist Richard Stallman Described Epstein Victims As 'Entirely Willing'|url=https://www.vice.com/en_us/article/9ke3ke/famed-computer-scientist-richard-stallman-described-epstein-victims-as-entirely-willing|access-date=2020-08-05|website=www.vice.com|language=en}}</ref> In the thread, discussion had turned to deceased MIT professor [[Marvin Minsky]], who was named by [[Virginia Giuffre]], a victim in the underage sex trafficking ring run by Epstein, as one of the people that Epstein had directed her to have sex with as a minor. In response to a comment where one reply stated that Minsky "is accused of assaulting one of Epstein's victims", Stallman defended Minsky by claiming that "the most plausible scenario is that she presented herself to him as entirely willing", and asserted that the use of "assault" or "rape" in Minsky's case should not apply when further challenged by other members of the mailing list.<ref name="arstech resignation">{{Cite web|last=Lee|first=Timothy B.|date=2019-09-17|title=Richard Stallman leaves MIT after controversial remarks on rape|url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/09/richard-stallman-leaves-mit-after-controversial-remarks-on-rape/|url-status=live|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-08-05|website=Ars Technica|language=en-us}}</ref> Stallman remained critical of Epstein and his role, stating "We know that Giuffre was being coerced into sex – by Epstein. She was being harmed."<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/education/2019/sep/17/mit-scientist-emails-epstein | title = MIT scientist resigns over emails discussing academic linked to Epstein | first = Victoria | last = Bekiempis | date = September 17, 2019 | accessdate = August 5, 2020 | work = [[The Guardian]] }}</ref>   

Stallman's posts were published to the public via [[Medium (website)|Medium]], and ''[[Vice (magazine)|Vice]]'' published a copy of the email chain to that point on September 13, 2019, drawing attention to Stallman's comments.<ref name="vice emailchain"/><ref name="wapost resignation">{{cite web | url = https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2019/09/17/computer-scientist-richard-stallman-resigns-mit-after-comments-about-epstein-scandal/ | title = Computer scientist Richard Stallman resigns from MIT after comments about Epstein scandal | first = Susan | last = Svrluga | date = September 13, 2019 | accessdate = August 5, 2020 |work  =[[The Washington Post]] }}</ref> Many people started to look into Stallman's past writings over the following days to find what were considered troubling statements related to underage sex and laws relating to child pornography from 2013 and earlier.<ref name="arstech resignation"/> Tied to his comments regarding Minsky, this led to several calling for Stallman's resignation.<ref name="wapost resignation"/><ref name="vice emailchain"/> Stallman acknowledged on September 14 that he had since learned his past writings that there were problems with underage sex, stating on his blog "Through personal conversations in recent years, I've learned to understand how sex with a child can harm her psychologically. This changed my mind about the matter: I think adults should not do that."<ref name="arstech resignation"/>

On September 16, Stallman announced his resignation from both MIT and FSF, "due to pressure on MIT and me over a series of misunderstandings and mischaracterizations".<ref name="Levy2019">{{cite web|url=https://www.wired.com/story/richard-stallman-and-the-fall-of-the-clueless-nerd/|title=Richard Stallman and the fall of the clueless nerd|first=Steven|last=Levy|publisher=Wired|date=September 18, 2019|accessdate=September 18, 2019}}</ref> In a post on his website, Stallman asserted that his posts to the email lists were not to defend Epstein, stating "Nothing could be further from the truth. I've called him a 'serial rapist,' and said he deserved to be imprisoned. But many people now believe I defended him—and other inaccurate claims—and feel a real hurt because of what they believe I said. I'm sorry for that hurt. I wish I could have prevented the misunderstanding."<ref name="arstech resignation"/> Stallman remains head of the [[GNU Project]].<ref name="phoronix.com"/>
