<span style="font-size:110%">'''Dispute overview'''</span>

The dispute revolves around the claim of Palermo being the capital of the Holy Roman Empire during the reign of Emperor Frederick II (1220–1254). The article currently removes Palermo from the list of capitals, even if it wasn't the initial request in the talk page.

Proponents of Palermo as the capital argue that, as both King of Sicily and Holy Roman Emperor, Frederick II’s governance was largely centered in Palermo, which held symbolic and administrative importance during his reign. Palermo was the capital of the Kingdom of Sicily, and while Frederick II spent time in various locations, Palermo remained the imperial capital due to its central role in administration, culture, and symbolism, including hosting Frederick's court and the Sicilian School of poetry.

On the other hand, opponents argue that Palermo was not a capital of the Holy Roman Empire, emphasizing that Frederick's reign was peripatetic, and his administrative functions were more focused on southern Italy, particularly in Foggia, which served as a functional royal seat and imperial base. They highlight the term “Regalis sede inclita imperialis” used in reference to Foggia as a royal residence, which they believe gives it greater significance than Palermo in the context of the empire.

The central issue at hand is the distinction between a capital as a symbolic and permanent seat of governance and a royal residence or administrative hub used temporarily for practical purposes. The debate is further complicated by the relationship between the Kingdom of Sicily and the Holy Roman Empire during Frederick's reign, with some arguing that the Kingdom of Sicily was part of the Empire, thus validating Palermo's role as its capital during this period.

The removal of Palermo without sufficient justification is also a concern of those advocating for its reinstatement.

<span style="font-size:110%">'''How have you tried to resolve this dispute before coming here?'''</span>

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Holy_Roman_Empire#Correction_of_Capital_of_the_Holy_Roman_Empire_(1220%E2%80%931254)

<span style="font-size:110%">'''How do you think we can help resolve the dispute?'''</span>

I believe the dispute can be resolved by reinstating Palermo as the capital of the Holy Roman Empire during Frederick II's reign (1194–1254). Palermo was the center of his administration, culture, and authority. It was where the royal court resided. This is supported by credible historical sources, and removing it as the capital without proper justification undermines the established historical consensus.

