{{quote|Bates wrote in 1936 that shortly before returning to Australia in 1899, The Times had accepted an offer of hers to investigate claims of cruel treatment of Aboriginal people in the north of Western Australia. She said she began this investigation shortly after arriving in Perth and that her findings had been published by The Times ... Her first known publication in The Times is from 1904 – a letter sent in response to an article by Walter Malcolmson published in the same year.}}

I have made the argument that the paragraph should be edited so that it says: "She said she began this investigation shortly after arriving in Perth and that her findings had been published in an <u>article</u>." A secondary source (De Vries, 2008, pg. 137) notes that she made this claim. This is relevant as the section's purpose is to note examples of her auto-biographical unreliability, which academics consider to be a source of uncertainty when it comes to evaluating her anthropological work.

I have also made the argument that the paragraph should be edited so that it includes: "The claims she said to have investigated were made by Walter Malcolmson." as two secondary sources (Reece, 2007, pg. 30; Lomas, 2015, pg. 46) agree that the claims that she said to have investigated (which she quotes in 1936) were made by Walter Malcolmson. This is relevant as it makes it clear that Bates was making a false claim as Malcolmson did not write these claims in The Times until 1904, years after Bates had returned to Western Australia.

Gawaon argues that Bates's 1936 semi-autobiography should be used over the secondary sources for the 1st disagreement as {{tq|"it should be possible to verify that she [wrote] that by consulting the primary source."}} For the 2nd, Gawaon believes Reece, a professional historian, has made a chronological error and that it should not be used as a source.

